CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --pointer-check --bounds-check
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* global placement: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* class allocation function: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] .* class deallocation function: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] .* inherited allocation: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] .* ::new uses the global: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.6\] .* ::delete uses the global: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.7\] .* array with cookie: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.8\] .* array deleted: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.9\] .* non-throwing allocation failed: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.10\] .* non-throwing allocation: SUCCESS$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The allocation function of a new-expression is found in the scope of the
class (also a base), else in the global scope, by overload resolution with
the placement arguments ([expr.new]/13, /20); `::new' and `::delete' search
the global scope only.  A non-throwing allocation function returning null
skips the initialization ([expr.new]/22).  Class-specific and global
placement allocation functions were ignored.
